# Design Specification: CVA6 Core Package (`ariane_pkg`)

## 1. Overview

This specification covers the SystemVerilog package defined in `core/include/ariane_pkg.sv`. It is a compile-time package. It has no hardware ports, clocks, resets, or state elements. Its externally observable behavior consists of the configuration values, type definitions, and pure helper functions that it exports to the rest of the core.

The package must provide a single, consistent set of:

- core configuration structures and default configuration values;
- elaboration-time parameters derived from the active build configuration;
- data types and encodings used by the frontend, issue, execution, memory, and debug units;
- pure combinational helper functions for address classification, immediate generation, byte-enable generation, data alignment, and instruction operation classification.

All helper functions in the package shall be stateless and shall not modify any global state.

---

## 2. Global Configuration Behavior

### 2.1 Configuration Record

The package must define a configuration record that carries the core-wide parameters needed by the microarchitecture. This record shall contain:

- return-address stack depth;
- branch-target buffer entry count;
- branch-history table entry count;
- physical-memory attribute rule counts for non-idempotent regions, executable regions, and cacheable regions;
- rule tables with a base address and length for each region;
- a bit indicating whether the core is used with a 64-bit AXI adapter;
- a bit indicating whether endianness should be swapped in the L1.5 OpenPiton adapter;
- debug-module base address;
- number of physical-memory-protection entries.

The rule tables shall support up to 16 rules per rule type. The active number of rules is part of the configuration. When performing region classification, only the first active rule count entries of each table shall be considered; unused entries shall be ignored.

### 2.2 Default Configuration

The package must provide a default configuration. The default values shall be derived from the active core configuration for frontend structure sizes and PMP entry count.

The default memory-attribute rules shall be:

- two non-idempotent rule slots, configured with zero-length regions;
- three executable regions:
  - DRAM starting at `0x8000_0000` with length `0x4000_0000`;
  - boot ROM starting at `0x1_0000` with length `0x10000`;
  - debug module starting at `0x0` with length `0x1000`;
- one cacheable region:
  - DRAM starting at `0x8000_0000` with length `0x4000_0000`.

The default configuration shall set:

- AXI-compliant mode enabled;
- endianness swap disabled;
- debug-module base address set to zero;
- PMP entry count taken from the active core configuration.

### 2.3 Configuration Validation

The configuration-validation function checks its argument using simulation assertions when called in builds where these assertions are enabled; these checks are excluded from synthesis and Verilator builds. The validation requirements are:

- the return-address stack depth must be greater than zero;
- branch-target buffer entry count must be a power of two;
- branch-history table entry count must be a power of two;
- each rule count must be less than or equal to 16;
- the PMP entry count must be less than or equal to 16.

The validation function shall not be required to perform these checks when assertions are disabled.

---

## 3. Derived Elaboration-Time Parameters

The package must define derived constants that control the core implementation. These constants shall be computed from the active configuration and from the RISC-V base ISA configuration.

### 3.1 Issue and Scoreboard Parameters

- The scoreboard entry count shall be taken from the core configuration.
- The transaction identifier width shall be the base-2 logarithm of the scoreboard entry count.
- The issue width shall be one.
- Commit-queue depth shall be four when a write-through data cache is enabled; otherwise it shall be eight.
- Register address width shall be six bits.
- General-purpose register file read ports shall be two.
- Writeback ports shall be four, or five when the custom execute interface is enabled.

### 3.2 Address Space and MMU Parameters

- The address-space identifier width shall be 16 bits for 64-bit operation and 1 bit for 32-bit operation.
- The MMU shall be present.
- Instruction TLB and data TLB entry counts shall be taken from the core configuration.
- The physical page number width for 4 KiB pages shall be 38 bits.

### 3.3 Floating-Point Parameters

The package must derive all floating-point configuration from the set of enabled floating-point extensions.

- Floating-point presence shall be true if any floating-point format extension is enabled.
- The widest floating-point format length shall be selected from the enabled formats:
  - 64 bits if double-precision is enabled;
  - otherwise 32 bits if single-precision is enabled;
  - otherwise 16 bits for half-precision formats;
  - otherwise 8 bits for quarter-precision;
  - otherwise 1 bit when no floating-point support is present.
- Non-standard extension presence shall be true if any half-precision, alternative half-precision, quarter-precision, or vector floating-point format is enabled.
- Vector floating-point availability for a given format shall require:
  - the scalar format itself to be enabled;
  - vector floating-point mode to be enabled;
  - the widest enabled floating-point format to be wider than the vector element format.

The package shall define fixed latency parameters for floating-point addition, multiplication, division, square root, conversion, and non-compute operations.

### 3.4 Instruction Fetch Parameters

- Fetch FIFO depth shall be four entries.
- Fetch width shall be 32 bits.
- When the compressed-extension is enabled, each fetch word shall carry up to two instructions; otherwise it shall carry one instruction.

### 3.5 Cache Geometry Parameters

The package must define instruction and data cache geometry:

- set associativity, line width, index width, and tag width for the instruction cache;
- set associativity, line width, index width, and tag width for the data cache;
- index width shall be the base-2 logarithm of the cache size divided by set associativity;
- tag width shall be the physical address width minus the index width;
- cache user-sideband widths shall be derived from the AXI user-sideband configuration.

In OpenPiton integration builds, cache geometry and floating-point configuration shall follow the OpenPiton L15 configuration; otherwise they shall follow the core configuration.

### 3.6 AXI User-Sideband Parameters

- If no AXI user sidebands are enabled, the AXI user width shall be 1.
- If user sidebands are enabled, the AXI user width shall be at least the data user width and at least twice the fetch user width.
- Data and fetch user-sideband enable bits and widths shall be taken from the core configuration.

### 3.7 Miscellaneous Execution Parameters

- The bit-manipulation extension enable bit shall be zero.
- Cache flush behavior shall invalidate cache lines when set to one, and may skip invalidation only in single-core systems when explicitly configured.
- In spike-tandem builds:
  - cycle counting shall be disabled;
  - WFI shall be treated as a no-op;
  - trap values shall be zeroed for all traps except memory faults.
- In normal builds:
  - cycle counting shall be enabled;
  - WFI shall be enabled;
  - trap values shall not be zeroed.

---

## 4. Type and Encoding Requirements

The package must define the data types and encodings used by the pipeline. These types are part of the package’s behavior because downstream units rely on them to interpret control information consistently.

### 4.1 Exception and Interrupt Types

- An exception record shall contain an XLEN-bit cause, an XLEN-bit trap value, and a one-bit valid field, in that packed order (2 × XLEN + 1 bits).
- An interrupt-control record shall contain interrupt-pending bits, interrupt-enable bits, interrupt-delegation bits, the supervisor interrupt-enable bit, and a global interrupt-enable bit.

### 4.2 Control-Flow Prediction Types

The package must define encodings for control-flow prediction kinds:

- no control-flow prediction;
- conditional branch;
- jump with immediate target;
- indirect jump / register target;
- return-address prediction.

The control-flow kind occupies three bits, with the listed kinds encoded consecutively from 0 through 4.

It must also define records for:

- branch-target-buffer updates and predictions;
- return-address-stack updates;
- branch-history-table updates and predictions;
- branch-resolution information containing validity, PC, target address, mispredict indication, taken indication, and control-flow kind.

### 4.3 Frontend and Fetch Types

The package must define:

- a two-bit instruction fetch exception encoding: no exception = 0, instruction access fault = 1, and instruction page fault = 2;
- fetch entry record containing the PC, instruction word, branch-prediction information, and any fetch exception;
- instruction fetch address-translation request and response records.

### 4.4 Functional Unit Types and Operation Codes

The package must define a functional-unit type with values for:

- no unit;
- load;
- store;
- ALU;
- control-flow;
- multiply/divide;
- CSR;
- floating-point;
- vector floating-point;
- custom execute interface.

The package must also define an operation-code enumeration covering all instruction classes that can be issued by the core. This shall include:

- integer arithmetic, logic, shifts, comparisons, and branches;
- jumps and register-indirect jumps;
- CSR operations and privilege instructions;
- load/store operations for all integer and floating-point widths;
- atomic memory operations;
- integer multiply and divide operations;
- floating-point arithmetic, fused operations, conversions, moves, compares, classify, and loads/stores;
- vectorial floating-point operations;
- custom execute-interface offload operations;
- bit-manipulation operations, including rotates, byte-reverse, sign/zero extension, carry-less multiply, single-bit operations, min/max, and shift-add forms.

### 4.5 Scoreboard Entry Type

The package must define a scoreboard entry record that carries, for each instruction in flight:

- PC;
- transaction identifier;
- functional unit and operation code;
- source and destination register addresses;
- operand/result value;
- immediate-use and PC-relative-use indicators;
- exception information;
- branch-prediction information;
- compressed-instruction indicator.

### 4.6 Atomic Memory Operation Encodings

The package must define atomic memory operation encodings for:

- no atomic operation;
- load-reserved;
- store-conditional;
- swap;
- add;
- and;
- or;
- xor;
- signed and unsigned max/min.

It must also define reserved compare-and-swap encodings. These reserved encodings are not part of the standard RISC-V atomic operation set and shall not be used as normal AMO operations.

### 4.7 TLB Update Types

The package must define TLB update records for both 64-bit and 32-bit address translation:

- a 64-bit format containing a valid bit, large-page indicators, VPN, ASID, and page-table entry content;
- a 32-bit format containing a valid bit, large-page indicator, VPN, ASID, and Sv32 page-table entry content.

### 4.8 Cache Request and Response Types

The package must define request and response records for instruction-cache and data-cache access. These records must carry the address, data, byte-enable, user-sideband, exception, and handshake information needed by the cache subsystem.

---

## 5. Pure Helper Function Behavior

The package shall provide the following helper functions. All functions are combinational and shall be usable from any part of the core.

### 5.1 Address Range Check

The range-check function shall return true if and only if:

```text
base <= address < base + length
```

The check must be performed without overflow. The base-plus-length computation shall be wide enough to represent one additional bit beyond the address width.

### 5.2 Region Classification

The package must provide region-classification functions for:

- non-idempotent regions;
- executable regions;
- cacheable regions.

For each classification:

- the function shall test the address against each active region rule;
- the final result shall be the logical OR of all active rule matches;
- rules beyond the active rule count shall be ignored.

If no region rules are active, the membership result for that classification shall be false.

### 5.3 Immediate Generation

The package must provide immediate-generation behavior for the standard RISC-V instruction formats:

- UJ-type immediate:
  - forms a signed PC-relative offset;
  - the least-significant bit shall be zero;
  - the offset shall be sign-extended to VLEN, the configured PC width.
- I-type immediate:
  - forms a 12-bit signed immediate;
  - shall be sign-extended to the configured virtual-address width VLEN.
- SB-type immediate:
  - forms a signed branch offset;
  - the least-significant bit shall be zero;
  - the offset shall be sign-extended to VLEN, the configured PC width.

### 5.4 Sign Extension

The package must provide a function that sign-extends a 32-bit value to the configured data width.

### 5.5 Byte-Enable Generation

The package must provide byte-enable generation for memory accesses.

For a 64-bit data path, given a byte address and an access size, the function must produce an 8-bit byte-enable vector:

- 8-byte accesses shall enable all eight bytes, independently of the supplied byte offset;
- 4-byte accesses shall enable the four consecutive byte lanes containing the addressed word;
- 2-byte accesses shall enable the two consecutive byte lanes containing the addressed halfword;
- 1-byte accesses shall enable only the addressed byte lane;
- for 4-byte and 2-byte accesses, a starting offset that would extend beyond the 8-byte granule shall enable no bytes. Alignment validation is performed by the caller.

For a 32-bit data path, byte-enable generation shall behave analogously within a 4-byte granule:

- 4-byte accesses shall enable all four bytes, independently of the supplied byte offset;
- 2-byte accesses shall enable two consecutive lanes starting at the supplied byte offset, for offsets 0 through 2;
- 1-byte accesses shall enable the addressed byte;
- a 2-byte access starting at offset 3 and an unsupported 8-byte size shall enable no bytes. Alignment validation is performed by the caller.

### 5.6 Data Alignment

The package must provide a data-alignment helper that maps a data word to the byte lanes required by a memory access at a given byte address. The alignment behavior must be consistent with the configured data path width and the byte-enable behavior.

For an access at byte offset A:

- the least-significant byte of the accessed data object shall be placed in byte lane A;
- the data word shall rotate left by 8 × A bits within XLEN, with bytes wrapping around the word;
- for a 64-bit data path, the byte offset is taken from the low three address bits;
- for a 32-bit data path, the effective byte offset is taken from the low two address bits.

### 5.7 Transfer-Size Extraction

The package must provide a function that maps an operation code to a two-bit memory transfer size (00 = byte, 01 = halfword, 10 = word, 11 = doubleword):

- 64-bit accesses for doubleword loads/stores and 64-bit AMOs;
- 32-bit accesses for word loads/stores and 32-bit AMOs;
- 16-bit accesses for halfword loads/stores;
- 8-bit accesses for byte loads/stores;
- doubleword size for operations not otherwise classified.

### 5.8 Floating-Point Operand Classification

The package must provide functions that classify whether each source or destination field of a floating-point instruction refers to the floating-point register file.

The classification requirements are:

- if floating-point support is not present, all classification functions shall return false;
- classification must cover floating-point loads/stores, computational operations, fused operations, conversions, moves, sign-injection, compare, classify, and vectorial floating-point operations;
- operations such as multiply-add that use three floating-point operands must be identified so that the third operand is handled correctly;
- non-floating-point operations shall return false.

### 5.9 Branch and Atomic Operation Classification

The package must provide:

- a branch-classification function that returns true for conditional branch comparison operations;
- an atomic-operation classification function that returns true for all atomic memory operations in the supported atomic encoding range.

---

## 6. ISA Identification and Debug Constants

The package must define the ISA and debug identification values used by the core.

### 6.1 Vendor and Architecture Identification

- The vendor identification value shall be 0x0602, the JEDEC manufacturer ID of OpenHW Group, zero-extended to XLEN.
- The architecture identification value shall be 3, zero-extended to XLEN.
- The implementation identification CSR reports zero; that CSR behavior is specified by the CSR register-file interface.

### 6.2 ISA Identification

The package must define an ISA code value that reports the supported RISC-V extensions to software through the machine ISA CSR.

The ISA code shall indicate:

- the base integer ISA width in the machine XLEN field;
- support for the integer multiply/divide extension;
- support for supervisor mode;
- support for user mode;
- support for the atomic extension only when enabled;
- support for the compressed extension only when enabled;
- support for single-precision floating-point only when enabled;
- support for double-precision floating-point only when enabled;
- non-standard extension presence only when such extensions are enabled.

Unsupported extension bits shall be zero.

### 6.3 Supervisor Status Masks

The package must define read and write masks for supervisor-visible machine status.

- The supervisor read mask shall expose the standard supervisor status fields, including interrupt-enable bits, previous-privilege bits, floating-point state, extension state, memory-privilege bits, and state-dirty bit.
- The supervisor write mask shall permit writes only to the supervisor-writable fields: supervisor interrupt enable, supervisor previous interrupt enable, supervisor previous privilege, floating-point state, supervisor user-memory access, and make-executable-readable.
- Bits outside the read mask shall read as zero.
- Bits outside the write mask shall be unaffected by supervisor writes.

### 6.4 Debug Hart Information

The package must define static debug hart information that reports:

- two debug scratch registers;
- debug data registers accessible through memory-mapped debug module addresses;
- the debug data register count and base address from the debug module definitions.

---

## 7. Summary of Behavioral Contract

- The package shall be purely declarative and shall not introduce sequential behavior.
- It shall provide a single, consistent configuration path for the entire core.
- All derived parameters must be consistent with the active configuration and with the RISC-V privilege and ISA specifications.
- All helper functions shall be deterministic and shall not depend on hidden state.
- All region classifications, immediate formations, byte-enable generations, and operation classifications shall match the encodings and behaviors described above.
- The package shall not require or imply specific external port connections; its behavior is limited to the definitions and functions it provides to the core.
